nch of times  you'll be able to find that peak so  here's I'm just going to give you a code  example for the hill climber because  it's the simplest of the ones that I'm  going to look at um I'm not actually  gonna go through a full code example for  each of these but I do have like a  github repository that has code examples  for everything that I'm talking about  so to initialize the Skyrim looting  problem I'm just taking in the number of  items in the knapsack and then I'm just  creating basically I'm just  pre-allocated a list of zeros and then  iterating one by one and basically just  flipping a coin as to whether I add it  or not  so this is kind of a dumb way to  initialize the knapsack but you know it  can work and as a simple mutation  function all I'm doing is just flipping  one bit so basically that means I'm  gonna randomly select an item and if it  is in the inventory I'm gonna remove it  if it's not in the inventory that I'm  gonna add it so here I'm just doing that  by like choosing an index  in the list and then swapping it and  finally the hillclimber itself so you  can see this is not that much code this  is a pretty big font on this slide and  it all fits these algorithms actually  don't really take that many lines of  code to write so here what I'm doing is  I'm taking in a number of steps so  that's how many times I'm trying to  generate a new solution the weights and  values that those are the fixed  parameters of the problem that I  mentioned before the maximum weight that  you can carry and a random seed for  reproducibility so first I'm going to  initialize my solution um then I'm gonna  calculate the fitness of it and then for  each step I'm going to make a small  change and that's my candidate solution  and then I'm just gonna compare the  Fitness's of those two and then I repeat  that for however many steps I'm running  it and return the best so the next one I  want to talk about is simulated  annealing this is actually pretty  similar to hill climbing but it has a  changing temperature so what does that  mean the temperature is essentially a  probability of accepting a bad step and  this is kind of based on the idea that  molecules at a hotter temperature tend  to move around more randomly whereas at  a colder temperature they're less likely  to move around and they'll only move  into a more stable configuration so at a  hot temperature we'll accept a lot of  bad steps and it's essentially a random  walk we'll make some change and we'll  just say yeah sure let's try that um at  a colder temperature it's more like a  hill climber you're not accepting as  many bad steps so you have this kind of  continuum at very hot temperatures it's  a random walk at very cold temperatures  it's a hill climber and the idea is that  you start at a fairly hot temperature to  explore the fitness landscape and over  time you're cooling the temperature and  so you're like honing in more and more  on promising areas of the fitness  landscape and so at the beginning if  this is your fitness function you could  have so it's just like a hill climber  where you only have one solution at a  time but in the early steps you're going  to be all over the place you're just  trying to  explore this whole landscape and find  promising areas as the temperature cools  down and you run it for longer you're  going to be honing in on these Peaks and  hopefully by the time you cooled it and  you're finished running the algorithm  you're fairly close to the optimum  solution although it's possible that  you'll still get stuck in a little  optimum so this is great because it  gives us both exploration and  exploitation  so exploration is traversing the fitness  landscape trying to find promising areas  and exploitation is working in a region  where you already have been and trying  to find the absolute best solution in  that region this is still really fast  because you only have one solution at a  time and really all the only overhead  you're adding is keeping track of the  temperature but there is more tuning so  there are cooling schedules you have to  figure out like how hot the temperature  you're starting at how cold you end it  how you change that temperature over  time you can potentially reheat it there  are lots of variants and there's like a  whole little cottage industry of people  writing papers about simulated annealing  and the best cooling schedules under  different conditions and for specific  problems probably your so your problem  doesn't fit exactly into one of these  very constrained problems that a paper  has been written about um that's why our  using one of these like random based  optimizers but yeah there is there's a  lot of tuning involved and so I just  wanted to get note about tuning which is  a it's hard you don't really know that  much about your fitness landscape so  it's hard to know a priori what is a  good cooling schedule or what's a good  mutation rate um so a reasonable way to  handle this is to do a grid search you  just have a few different things you  want to try out for each parameter that  you're tuning and then you try each  combination of those so this is kind of  unfortunate this is actually like one of  the more difficult parts of these  algorithms because the structure the  algorithms themselves is fairly simple  so that's just kind of how it is so  finally I  evolutionary algorithms was in the title  so now I'm gonna talk about that what  I'm talking about right now is basically  a genetic algorithm but the structure is  generally the same across different  evolutionary algorithms so what makes  evolutionary algorithms different from  these other strategies is that you have  a population of solutions um so you're  probably keeping these in some kind of  list so here here's your population each  of these squares represents a solution  and let's say that the optimum is like a  warm color so we're trying to select  warmer colored solutions so first  there's a selection step um so you go  from this population and you want to  choose an individual to be a parent so  the basic flow of this algorithm is  you're going to replace this population  with child populate with a population of  children um which are closely related to  the parent population and you're just  going to evolve the whole population  through time kind of following this  biological idea of evolution like slow  gradual change of a population through  time so what does the selection look  like um a really good selection  algorithm is tournament selection and  this is really simple basically you just  choose some number of candidates I  personally recommend too and the best of  those candidates becomes a parent so  just to give you a sense of what the  inputs look like the fit fits is a list  of Fitness's that are associated with  like a list of solutions say and here  I'm setting the tournament size to be 2  so it's just the number of candidates in  the tournament and basically I'm gonna  sample from all of the different from  all those different Fitness's and those  are my competitors then I'm gonna get  their Fitness's and whichever one has  the maximum so I'm treating this as a  maximization problem is the winner and I  return that index and so then I'm gonna  take that parent solution and I'm gonna  mutate it or recombine it to create a  child and so you can see it's gonna be  fairly similar  to the parent but you can see it has a  slightly different fitness because we've  changed it a little bit so mutation I've  already talked about recombination is a  little bit more complicated the idea is  that you take multiple parents and you  take aspects of each of those solutions  so you might take the whatever the first  parent has like the first three items  and then the second three items from the  next parent and then the third step is  repopulation so you repeat these first  two steps once for every individual in  the population until you've completely  replaced the parent population and  that's one step so you can see if this  was the parent population before maybe  in the next generation it looks a little  bit more homogeneous because the better  solutions have been chosen more times  and so they are the parents for more of  the child solution in the next step and  then over time the population as a whole  is going to start off exploring  different areas in the fitness landscape  and over time it's going to converge to  the most promising areas and exploit  those areas so there's some major  advantages to this first off you're  unlikely to get stuck in a single local  optimum because you're automatically  exploring a lot of areas at once I also  think this connection to biology is just  really cool and kind of appealing but  there are some disadvantages too so it  could be easy to lose variation quickly  if your selection is too strong there's  also more tuning involved so you have to  choose a selection function you have to  choose a mutation function and a  recombination function you have to  choose the selection strength the  population size mutation size and all of  these things can have either very small  or very large effect on the quality of  your final output and it's hard to know  in advance what's gonna have a large  effect although I found a lot of times  like how you're mutating has a really  huge effect on the quality of the  solution so this is probably something  to spend a little bit more time tuning  so you can also see this algorithm is  going to be a lot slower than a hill  climber right you have to maintain an  entire population and you have to  Fitness is a lot you're you have this  whole process for a selection and then  you have to mutate you have to keep  track of a lot it's also more memory  hungry because you're maintaining all  these solutions in memory and so if each  solution takes up a lot of space and  have a large population size this can  slow things down more it can create its